From: Artem Bambalov <artembambalov1993@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] systemui: the wallpaper listens to the display, not the window

The static wallpaper draws into a fixed-size surface, and the only code
that ever resizes that surface is its own drawFrame. The system merely
delivers the occasion: a rotation puts the window into the resize
report, the report becomes onSurfaceRedrawNeeded, and drawFrame reads
the new display shape and asks for the matching surface. Miss the
report and the chain has no second chance -- a fixed-size window keeps
its old frame across the rotation, so no onSurfaceChanged arrives
either, and on a still launcher (offsets constant, visibility steady)
nothing calls drawFrame again. The surface stays shaped for the
previous orientation, and the panel shows the difference as a black
stripe: the stock wallpaper bitmap is 1920 to the panel's 2048, a
128px shortfall on whichever axis the stale shape leaves uncovered.

The report does get missed. A second rotation arriving mid-animation
is deferred, and when it finally lands the wallpaper window can sit
outside the layout pass that builds the resize list. Caught live on
device: portrait panel, landscape-shaped surface, and the engine's own
dump still holding the previous rotation -- drawFrame was simply never
invoked. Restarts don't heal it, because the window manager recreates
the surface from the recorded requested size.

Upstream fixed this line of code only by leaving it: Q rewrote the
wallpaper around a GL engine. The Q-era guard extension (comparing
remembered display dimensions, 4fcb6ec0ba98) does not help here --
P keeps no client-side DisplayInfo cache (USE_CACHE is false), the
window manager pushes rotation and logical size into the display
service atomically, so the size clause can never fire apart from the
rotation clause. The failure is a lost trigger, not stale data.

So take the trigger from the one source that always announces the
change: the display itself. A DisplayListener on the default display
calls drawFrame after every display change; the callback reads through
the uncached path, so it always sees the fresh shape, and the existing
guard does the rest. The extra calls on ordinary rotations fall into
drawFrame's own suppression. WallpaperService already runs one such
listener per engine for visibility reporting; a second registration
changes nothing for other consumers.

diff --git a/packages/SystemUI/src/com/android/systemui/ImageWallpaper.java b/packages/SystemUI/src/com/android/systemui/ImageWallpaper.java
index 50d862dee29..ccdb0648267 100644
--- a/packages/SystemUI/src/com/android/systemui/ImageWallpaper.java
+++ b/packages/SystemUI/src/com/android/systemui/ImageWallpaper.java
@@ -23,6 +23,7 @@ import android.graphics.Canvas;
 import android.graphics.Rect;
 import android.graphics.RectF;
 import android.graphics.Region.Op;
+import android.hardware.display.DisplayManager;
 import android.os.AsyncTask;
 import android.os.Handler;
 import android.os.Trace;
@@ -87,6 +88,28 @@ public class ImageWallpaper extends WallpaperService {
         private Display mDefaultDisplay;
         private final DisplayInfo mTmpDisplayInfo = new DisplayInfo();
 
+        // A fixed-size wallpaper surface keeps its frame across a display change,
+        // so no onSurfaceChanged arrives, and when the rotation's resize report
+        // misses this window nothing else is guaranteed to call drawFrame again.
+        // The display itself is the one source that always announces the change.
+        private final DisplayManager.DisplayListener mDisplayListener =
+                new DisplayManager.DisplayListener() {
+            @Override
+            public void onDisplayAdded(int displayId) {
+            }
+
+            @Override
+            public void onDisplayRemoved(int displayId) {
+            }
+
+            @Override
+            public void onDisplayChanged(int displayId) {
+                if (mDefaultDisplay != null && displayId == mDefaultDisplay.getDisplayId()) {
+                    drawFrame();
+                }
+            }
+        };
+
         boolean mVisible = true;
         boolean mOffsetsChanged;
         int mLastXTranslation;
@@ -129,6 +152,8 @@ public class ImageWallpaper extends WallpaperService {
 
             //noinspection ConstantConditions
             mDefaultDisplay = getSystemService(WindowManager.class).getDefaultDisplay();
+            getSystemService(DisplayManager.class).registerDisplayListener(mDisplayListener,
+                    getMainThreadHandler());
             setOffsetNotificationsEnabled(false);
 
             updateSurfaceSize(surfaceHolder, getDefaultDisplayInfo(), false /* forDraw */);
@@ -137,6 +162,7 @@ public class ImageWallpaper extends WallpaperService {
         @Override
         public void onDestroy() {
             super.onDestroy();
+            getSystemService(DisplayManager.class).unregisterDisplayListener(mDisplayListener);
             mBackground = null;
             unloadWallpaper(true /* forgetSize */);
         }
